nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BWest: ^*NPlease explain why I should bid 5@H.|
PG| ***** 32 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As to West's question as to why to bid 5@H. It is possible we are going down in 4@S losing 1 heart and 3 clubs though it is unlikely. |
PG| ***** 33 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|And if East has as little as KQxxxx of hearts, EW could lose 1 heart, 1 spade, and 3 diamonds for down 3. That is better than letting us make 4@S. Plus it might talk us into bidding 5@S.|
PG| ***** 34 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It does turn out because of the bad heart break, EW lose 2 hearts and 2 diamond and 1 spade for down 3. But even if doubled better than letting us make 4@S.|
PG| ***** 35 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^B@2^*BWest: ^*NI thought the 5 level always for the opponents so never bid 6 or 4 unless void or Ace.|
PG| ***** 36 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|There is a bridge saying that ^*BThe 5 Level Belongs to the Opponents^*n and that is a very good saying. |
PG| ***** 37 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, what it means is that it is right more often than not to avoid bidding ^*B5 over 5^*n meaning if the opponents are at the 5 level, you should avoid bidding over them at the 5 level most of the time.

On the other hand, ^*BEast^*n, when they are at the 4 level, as here then bidding 5 over 4 is often correct or at least more often. Avoid 5 over 5 but not necessarily 5 over 4.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BNorth: ^*NSo with that said if West bids 5@H then would you bid 5@S with my hand?|
PG| ***** 39 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Yes, if you have a void in their suit, 5 over 5 is usually right. With a doubleton, 5 over 5 is often wrong. With a singleton it is not clear. |
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Any length in their suit is the most important determinant in deciding whether or not to bid 5 over 5 although in general, most players bid 5 over 5 when they should not.|
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


